  <h2 id="overview">Health insurance for students in Germany: what you need to know</h2>

  <p>Every student enrolled at a German university must have valid health insurance. This is a legal requirement — you cannot complete your enrollment (Immatrikulation) without proof of coverage. The university registrar will ask for either a public health insurance membership certificate or a private health insurance exemption certificate before you can register.</p>

  <p>Most students in Germany are covered by the statutory student public health insurance rate, which is heavily subsidised and well below standard public health insurance contributions. Private health insurance for students is available but is only the right choice in specific situations.</p>

  <h2 id="student-gkv">Student public health insurance — the standard choice</h2>

  <p>Students under 30 (or in their first 14 semesters) who are enrolled at a German university qualify for the student public health insurance tariff. In 2025, this costs approximately <strong>€120–130 per month</strong>, including nursing care insurance (Pflegeversicherung).</p>

  <p>This rate applies regardless of whether you work part-time during your studies, as long as your income stays below the "Geringfügigkeitsgrenze" (currently €538/month). It covers all essential healthcare including GP visits, specialist referrals, hospital stays, and prescriptions.</p>

  <p>For most students — especially those under 25, healthy, and on limited budgets — student public health insurance is the simplest and most cost-effective option. It requires no health declaration and cannot be refused for pre-existing conditions.</p>

  <h2 id="student-pkv">When student private health insurance makes sense</h2>

  <p>Student private health insurance tariffs exist specifically for enrolled students and offer private-level coverage at a reduced premium — typically between €80 and €160 per month depending on the insurer and tariff selected.</p>

  <p>Private health insurance makes sense for students in the following situations:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Civil servant trainees (Beamtenanwärter)</strong> — If you are training for civil service, your employer pays a Beihilfe subsidy covering 50-70% of healthcare costs. Private health insurance is designed to complement this, and student private health insurance tariffs exist for this group.</li>
    <li><strong>Students over 30</strong> — Once you exceed the age limit for student public health insurance rates, standard public health insurance contributions apply (much higher). At this point, private health insurance becomes financially competitive.</li>
    <li><strong>High-income working students</strong> — If your part-time income regularly exceeds the Geringfügigkeitsgrenze, you may lose access to subsidised student public health insurance rates. Private health insurance can be a better deal.</li>
    <li><strong>Students who want private hospital access</strong> — Student public health insurance covers standard ward treatment. Private health insurance provides access to private rooms, chief physician treatment, and shorter waiting times.</li>
    <li><strong>International students with existing private health insurance from abroad</strong> — Some international students come with international health insurance that can be accepted as an equivalent. Check with your university's enrollment office.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="age-limit">The age 30 rule</h2>

  <p>The student public health insurance rate is only available until you turn 30. After your 30th birthday (or after your 14th semester, whichever comes first), you lose access to the discounted student rate and must pay standard public health insurance contributions — around €380–420 per month in 2025, depending on your income.</p>

  <p>At this point, many students find that student private health insurance tariffs (typically €100–160/month) become significantly cheaper than standard public health insurance. If you are approaching 30 and still a student, it is worth getting a private health insurance quote before your birthday.</p>

  <p>Important: if you switch to private health insurance as a student, you will eventually need to transition to a standard adult private health insurance tariff after graduation. Discuss this transition with your broker — the terms vary between insurers.</p>

  <h2 id="international">International students in Germany</h2>

  <p>If you are an international student from outside the EU/EEA, you need to provide proof of health insurance to receive your student visa and complete university enrollment. Your options are:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Enroll in German student public health insurance</strong> — Most international students do this. You apply directly with a public health insurer (TK, AOK, Barmer are common choices) and receive a membership certificate within a few days.</li>
    <li><strong>Student private health insurance tariff</strong> — Available to international students, but requires a health declaration. Some insurers are more flexible than others for non-EU nationals. A broker can identify which insurers are most suitable.</li>
    <li><strong>International student health insurance</strong> — Some universities accept recognized international health insurance plans as proof of coverage. Check with your specific university before purchasing any policy.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For students from EU/EEA countries: your European Health Insurance Card (EHIC) is not sufficient for enrollment in Germany. You need either public health insurance membership or a private health insurance policy.</p>

  <h2 id="working">Working students and income rules</h2>

  <p>Many students work part-time during their studies. The health insurance implications depend on how much you earn:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Under €538/month (Minijob)</strong> — No impact on student public health insurance eligibility. You pay no additional contributions.</li>
    <li><strong>€538–1,131/month (Midijob)</strong> — You may be required to pay income-related public health insurance contributions on top of the student rate, or transition to a regular employee contribution calculation.</li>
    <li><strong>Over €1,131/month regularly</strong> — You lose student public health insurance eligibility and must pay standard employee contributions. At this income level, private health insurance may be worth comparing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="after-graduation">What happens after you graduate</h2>

  <p>Your student health insurance — whether public or private health insurance — ends when you graduate or when your enrollment ends. What happens next depends on which system you were in:</p>

  <p><strong>If you were in student public health insurance:</strong> You transition to standard public health insurance as an employee when you start your first job. If your first job salary exceeds the income threshold (€77,400/year), you can immediately choose to switch to private health insurance.</p>

  <p><strong>If you were in student private health insurance:</strong> You transition to an adult private health insurance tariff. Your entry age premium is calculated based on your age at the time of transition. Starting private health insurance young — even at student rates — locks in a lower premium base for life.</p>

  <p>This is one of the most compelling arguments for students considering private health insurance: entering the system at 22 or 24 establishes a premium base far lower than entering at 32 or 35 after graduation.</p>

  <h2 id="checklist">Student insurance checklist</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Confirm whether your university requires public health insurance membership certificate or accepts private health insurance exemption</li>
    <li>Check your age — if you are under 30, student public health insurance is almost always simpler</li>
    <li>If over 30, get private health insurance quotes before paying standard public health insurance contributions</li>
    <li>International students: check your visa requirements before choosing a plan</li>
    <li>If you plan to work significantly during studies, calculate the income thresholds carefully</li>
    <li>Think about post-graduation plans — if you expect a high salary job, entering private health insurance as a student can save significantly over time</li>
  </ul>

